  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 219 AM CDT Sun Aug 9 2026\n\nBroad quasi-zonal flow is present across the CONUS this morning. \nBetween slightly above average 500 mb heights to our south and below \naverage heights to our north, the core of the jet to our north is on \nthe stronger side for early August. Several weak perturbations \nwithin the jet continue to move through the flow. One of these weak \nperturbations is moving across the central Plains right now. \nIsentropic ascent around 600 mb has allowed some widely scattered \nshowers and weak storms to develop from Manhattan to Marysville. \nThese will move eastward across the area through sunrise. Impacts \nwill remain low given the weak and high-based nature of the \nconvection, though with lots of low-level dry air can't completely \nrule out some brief strong gusts with any decaying storm. Persistent \nWAA into the afternoon hints that mid-level clouds may hang on a bit \nlonger than guidance suggests, but do think that clouds will clear \nat some point. Once this does occur, a breezy but hot and humid day \nis in store. Deeper mixing should support temperatures in the 100s \ntowards central KS, while farther east temperatures will be cooler \nbut dewpoints higher. As a result, have gone with a Heat Advisory for\nall but a few counties near the Nebraska border, as heat indices in \nmost spots should climb into the mid/upper 100s.\n\nThe next few days appear to be a classic \"dirty ridge\" pattern. \nSeveral more weak upper-level perturbations will move across the top \nof the ridge. Guidance is very consistent in a deep layer of \nsaturation and isentropic ascent above 600 mb, particularly during \nthe daytime hours Monday. Dry, inverted V profiles will be present \nbelow this layer. This is suggestive of thick mid/high level clouds \nwith scattered high-based convection. Any precipitation should be \nquite light (mainly in the form of sprinkles), so the main impact \nwill be for temperatures to be held in check where cloud cover is \nthickest. In similar past setups, guidance has struggled with \nboundary layer temperatures, and is typically on the warm end of \nwhat actually occurs. So have hedged slightly towards the cool side \nof guidance for temps/heat indices Monday, and even this may be \noverdone if high clouds stay thick all afternoon. The signal for \npersistent mid and high clouds and perhaps a few shower/storms \ncontinues Tuesday and Wednesday to a lesser extent. If skies stay \nmostly clear then temperatures in the mid/upper 90s with heat \nindices around 105 would be probable. But the potential for \ncontinued clouds suggests the heat could be reduced slightly - \nthough still rather warm.\n\nThursday into Friday, guidance still indicates a stronger shortwave \ntrough dropping southeast across the Great Lakes, pushing a front \nsouthward towards Kansas. Some variation remains in how far south \nthe front will get before it stalls out, with the GFS taking it into \nsoutheast Kansas, the ECMWF stalling it out around I-70, and most \nother guidance in between. Exact temperatures and precipitation \ncoverage will be dependent on where it does end up. But regardless, \nshower and storm chances will increase along and behind the boundary \nfor the second half of the week. Temperatures should range from the \n80s north of the front to still in the 90s south of the front.",
